A||N||4||Seanh||Why Does my Modem do this???||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||02-04-2001||05:54 PM||||On my new laptop I got for X-mas, it has a 56K modem. When I use it at home, it only connects usally around 36K, or in that area. I take it to my friends house, and it will connect around 46K. Then take it to work and it runs about 42K. Now today at home, it is only connected at 26K. Why the heck does it do this? I use the same phone cord where ever I take it. Is this normal? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto (3.4 Conversion in Progress)<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Notchit||02-04-2001||05:58 PM||||Its your phone lines. On my computer, i have a p3 500 with 128 mb of ram ect. and a 56k modem. I connect at 24,000 bps. when i first got the computer, i was pretty mad, if i wanted 24,000 bps i would have bought a 28.8 k modem. so i took it to a friends, and hooked it up to his phone line, it was around 48,000 bps. so it is your phoneline.<P>------------------<BR>Seein Red<BR>'85 Fiero GT<BR>ICQ#: 56708070<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Outside_side2(sml2).jpg"> <BR>_________________________||||reg||4
Z||000002||rogergarrison||02-04-2001||06:30 PM||||the 56k means thats only what its capable of. the line and server determine how fast it acctually is. my PCM cell phone only connects at 14k (talk about slow) I think that what all the dsl stuff is. a friend has what they call Roadrunner on his cable internet hookup, its like 250K.||||reg||1
Z||000003||pherder||02-04-2001||07:06 PM||||The main difference is how far the phone line is from a Central Office (Switching office).  The further from one, the lower the connect speed.<P>Example ... there is a Central office of SWBell in the town I live it.  When I was with SWBell, my modem would connect at 43333 or higher.<P>I fired SWBell and switched to another phone company and all I can get now is 33333 on a good day with the wind behind me!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  The new company's central office is in a different city so I am stuck with the slow speed <B>IF</B> I need to use the modem.<P>Actually I have a cable modem now and don't worry about connection speed since it is over 1 MBit download and ~750 KBit upload.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul<BR>------------------<BR>88 Formula<BR>88 Coupe<BR>86 SE<BR>95 GMC<BR>92 Mazda<BR>55 Jones<BR>Sedgwick, KS 67135<BR>pherder@kscable.com<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 02-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||02-05-2001||01:08 AM||||Fastest allowable speed on a dial up modem is 53k.  Some of it could depend on the traffic through your ISP.  I can usually log on at about 52k and change, but sometimes I can't go any faster than 25-30k.  Can't wait for DSL.||||reg||1
